1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
It rewrote the rules of aerial combat and left an indelible mark on aviation history.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
An incomparable combination of aerodynamic agility and lethal firepower.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:17,000
The Grumman F-14 Tomcat ruled the skies for nearly four decades.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, join me as we bid farewell to this most capable of Cold War fighters.

5
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:33,240
Welcome to the special edition for Modern Morbals, and the final farewell to the F-14

6
00:00:33,241 --> 00:00:33,960
Tomcat.

7
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:07,000
In 2006, an exalted but aging veteran of the skies wrote the final chapter in its illustrious

8
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:08,500
history.

9
00:01:08,501 --> 00:01:15,720
The last 22 operational F-14 Tomcats, based aboard the USS Theodore Roosevelt in the Persian

10
00:01:15,721 --> 00:01:21,620
Gulf, flew their final combat sorties over Iraq.

11
00:01:21,621 --> 00:01:26,820
would be their last flights into harm's way before their scheduled retirement from active duty.

12
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:39,540
Now, months later, in the waters just off the coast of Virginia,

13
00:01:39,541 --> 00:01:45,220
the last two squadrons of F-14s are about to complete their last aircraft carrier exercise

14
00:01:45,940 --> 00:01:46,820
and make for home.

15
00:01:51,620 --> 00:01:56,620
This is the flight deck of the USS Theodore Roosevelt.

16
00:01:56,621 --> 00:01:59,620
We're here today to document the last cats and traps,

17
00:01:59,621 --> 00:02:02,620
or catapult takeoffs and arrested landings,

18
00:02:02,621 --> 00:02:05,620
of one of the Navy's most amazing fighter jets,

19
00:02:05,621 --> 00:02:07,620
the F-14 Tomcat.

20
00:02:10,620 --> 00:02:12,620
Now, I had the distinct privilege

21
00:02:12,621 --> 00:02:15,620
of driving the Tomcat for six years.

22
00:02:15,621 --> 00:02:18,620
The first three were with Fighter Squadron 51

23
00:02:18,621 --> 00:02:21,620
embarked aboard the USS Carl Vincen.

24
00:02:21,621 --> 00:02:24,620
Back then, our commanding officer was a sharp aviator

25
00:02:24,621 --> 00:02:28,620
named Admiral Robert F. Willard, callsign Ratt.

26
00:02:28,621 --> 00:02:32,620
First time I flew the airplane, the airplane was fairly new.

27
00:02:32,621 --> 00:02:36,620
I was one of the early guys that came out of the training command

28
00:02:36,621 --> 00:02:39,620
into this jet that was bigger than life.

29
00:02:39,621 --> 00:02:42,620
Admiral Willard is now the Vice Chief of Naval Operations,

30
00:02:42,621 --> 00:02:47,620
but like me, he'll always be a Tomcat guy at heart.

31
00:02:48,620 --> 00:02:57,539
It was about the stick, and it was about the rudders, and it was about your ability to move those big surfaces in the air to make the airplane do what we wanted it to

32
00:02:57,540 --> 00:02:58,240
do.

33
00:02:58,241 --> 00:03:08,080
And what I'll miss the most is the athletic part of flying the F-14 and the athletic part of fighting another one.

34
00:03:08,081 --> 00:03:15,940
Now, my last three years in the Navy was spent at the West Coast Replacement Air Group,

35
00:03:15,941 --> 00:03:19,960
or RAG, where I instructed new pilots in the Tomcat.

36
00:03:19,961 --> 00:03:24,060
Nowadays, I'm an airline pilot for American Airlines.

37
00:03:24,061 --> 00:03:28,980
But looking back, I can honestly say being strapped into the cockpit of the incredible

38
00:03:28,981 --> 00:03:35,800
F-14 Tomcat was one of the best times of my life.

39
00:03:35,801 --> 00:03:40,800
After 31 years of frontline service in our country's defense, the F-14 is headed to the

40
00:03:40,801 --> 00:03:41,980
Boneyard.

41
00:03:41,981 --> 00:03:46,900
But before it does, we'll gain some in-depth perspective from those who fly it and maintain

42
00:03:46,901 --> 00:03:51,460
it both on the beach and here, what is affectionately known as the boat.

43
00:03:51,461 --> 00:03:59,280
So please, strap in and welcome aboard.

44
00:03:59,281 --> 00:04:03,720
Now some of my naval comrades have described an aircraft carrier flight deck as being the

45
00:04:03,721 --> 00:04:08,580
most dangerous four and a half acres of U.S. real estate anywhere in the world.

46
00:04:08,581 --> 00:04:14,880
I'd have to agree and the USS Theodore Roosevelt is no exception.

47
00:04:14,881 --> 00:04:20,280
It led the coalition strike group in Operation Enduring Freedom in 2001 and

48
00:04:20,281 --> 00:04:25,960
in 2003 for Operation Iraqi Freedom.

49
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:33,360
With an arsenal of approximately 70 tactical aircraft, the Roosevelt

50
00:04:33,361 --> 00:04:35,360
chalked up some astounding stats.

51
00:04:36,060 --> 00:04:39,380
1,703 combat sorties flown,

52
00:04:39,960 --> 00:04:42,740
11 million gallons of jet fuel consumed,

53
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:46,040
and more than 1 million pounds of ordnance dropped.

54
00:04:46,580 --> 00:04:50,640
And the F-14 Tomcat played an integral role in that equation.

55
00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:56,200
It really has evolved from sort of a Cold War icon

56
00:04:56,201 --> 00:04:59,400
into a modern-day lethality air-to-ground bomber.

57
00:04:59,760 --> 00:05:01,580
When we incorporated the Lantern system,

58
00:05:01,581 --> 00:05:04,220
laser targeting pod onto the plane,

59
00:05:04,221 --> 00:05:08,260
and some other capabilities that have evolved over time.

60
00:05:08,261 --> 00:05:11,260
It's truly become, I think, the weapon of choice

61
00:05:11,261 --> 00:05:13,100
in Iraq right now.

62
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:18,500
Navy Commander Jim Howe is the commanding officer of VF-31,

63
00:05:18,501 --> 00:05:20,760
better known as the Tomcatters,

64
00:05:20,761 --> 00:05:24,800
one of two F-14 squadrons still aboard the Teddy Roosevelt.

65
00:05:24,801 --> 00:05:27,580
But soon, like the rest of his squadron,

66
00:05:27,581 --> 00:05:29,580
the skipper will return to the States

67
00:05:29,581 --> 00:05:34,780
to begin training in the Tomcats replacement, the F-18 Super Hornet.

68
00:05:36,380 --> 00:05:40,800
One of the things you've mentioned before is this whole transition is bittersweet.

69
00:05:40,940 --> 00:05:42,100
Can you touch upon that?

70
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:42,960
Absolutely.

71
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:50,080
As a career F-14 pilot, it's been just a great joy of mine to fly it.

72
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:54,980
And I think the thing I'll miss most are the people associated with it.

73
00:05:54,981 --> 00:05:59,940
It's truly my feeling that the best people I know either fly or fix this aircraft.

74
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:07,640
It takes a myriad of sailors to safely get a Tomcat on and off the flight deck.

75
00:06:07,641 --> 00:06:12,380
It may look like chaos, but it truly is a well-choreographed ballet.

76
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:18,220
Each sailor, identified by their colored jersey, has a specific job to do.

77
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:21,440
Blue jerseys tow the aircraft into place.

78
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:24,840
Purple jerseys are responsible for fuel.

79
00:06:24,980 --> 00:06:29,980
Red jerseys handle aircraft weapons and crash safety.

80
00:06:29,981 --> 00:06:33,980
Green provides aircraft and arresting gear maintenance.

81
00:06:33,981 --> 00:06:38,980
Yellow are in charge of traffic and setting the steam catapult for takeoff.

82
00:06:38,981 --> 00:06:42,980
White are safety and landing signal officers.

83
00:06:42,981 --> 00:06:46,980
And finally, the brown jerseys or plane captains.

84
00:06:46,981 --> 00:06:53,980
They are the last set of eyes, ears and hands on the aircraft before the pilot straps in.

85
00:06:53,981 --> 00:06:58,580
And I won't be the only pilot to tell you how important these sailors are to the team.

86
00:06:59,640 --> 00:07:02,680
We are the last line of defense, as you say.

87
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:04,280
Remember one problem we have?

88
00:07:04,520 --> 00:07:05,300
Magnets flying?

89
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:06,260
FOD.

90
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:08,080
You got FOD on our intakes.

91
00:07:08,940 --> 00:07:11,780
The most important job we do is make sure our intakes are good and clean,

92
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:15,440
make sure there's no troubles, no loose missing fasteners or anything.

93
00:07:15,441 --> 00:07:17,180
Got to make sure all our screws are there.

94
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:18,680
None are loose, none are missing.

95
00:07:18,681 --> 00:07:25,440
FOD is a serious threat for any aircraft on a carrier.

96
00:07:26,020 --> 00:07:30,120
Loose objects like a stray tool or even the smallest bolt left on the deck

97
00:07:30,121 --> 00:07:35,220
could potentially be sucked up into the jet intakes and have devastating consequences.

98
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:41,180
Once a plane captain has checked and cleared the intakes, he must then secure the cockpit.

99
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:46,360
Before y'all get in, we set up the cockpit and make sure all the lap belts and everything are set and ready to go.

100
00:07:46,460 --> 00:07:47,960
All your switches are in good position.

101
00:07:47,961 --> 00:07:51,680
That way when we put power on, y'all get up there, you're ready to go, and get you launched

102
00:07:51,681 --> 00:07:54,140
off the deck quick and in a hurry.

103
00:07:54,141 --> 00:07:59,420
Totally put my trust in these guys, because once you're going off the front end of the

104
00:07:59,421 --> 00:08:03,760
catapult, that's it.

105
00:08:03,761 --> 00:08:08,140
The F-14 squadrons here on the Roosevelt have been working together as a team for a long

106
00:08:08,141 --> 00:08:09,140
time.

107
00:08:09,141 --> 00:08:14,940
Only words like faith, respect, and professionalism can truly describe the attitude they share

108
00:08:14,941 --> 00:08:16,400
for one another.

109
00:08:16,401 --> 00:08:19,700
Retiring the Tomcat in the coming days will not be easy.

110
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:23,580
Are the troops happy or sad to see the F-14 go?

111
00:08:23,720 --> 00:08:25,060
I think everybody's sad to see it go.

112
00:08:25,300 --> 00:08:26,860
The flight deck guys, they just love it.

113
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:28,520
It's a real fighter. Makes a ton of noise.

114
00:08:29,460 --> 00:08:31,900
And you feel like you're making things happen there on the flight deck.

115
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:35,520
So everybody's going to be sad to see it go from top to bottom of this ship

116
00:08:35,521 --> 00:08:37,520
and probably in naval aviation as well.

117
00:08:38,060 --> 00:08:38,880
So we'll miss it.

118
00:08:40,480 --> 00:08:43,340
These squadrons, they wanted to be here on this last deployment

119
00:08:43,341 --> 00:08:45,100
with both F-14 squadrons that we had here,

120
00:08:45,101 --> 00:08:51,080
VF-31 and VF-213, and they worked extremely hard, and the squadrons did well.

121
00:08:51,180 --> 00:08:52,920
They led the air wing in statistics.

122
00:08:53,160 --> 00:08:57,380
They had 100% sortie completion rate on deployment on their combat sorties.

123
00:08:58,680 --> 00:09:01,920
It's now the final hour for the Tomcats here on the Roosevelt.

124
00:09:02,600 --> 00:09:05,180
Several F-14s have already begun heading home.

125
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:10,660
The remaining pilots and their radar intercept officers, or REOs,

126
00:09:10,661 --> 00:09:15,040
must now suit up for their very last catapult carrier takeoff.

127
00:09:15,100 --> 00:09:19,100
Commander Kurt Seth is one of the last pilots standing.

128
00:09:19,101 --> 00:09:22,100
Do you have any thoughts on the last Tomcat flyoff?

129
00:09:22,101 --> 00:09:26,100
No, it's always exciting leaving the boat, going home, seeing your family.

130
00:09:26,101 --> 00:09:31,100
Unfortunately, it's the last time the Tomcat's leaving the carrier.

131
00:09:31,101 --> 00:09:35,100
It's pretty bittersweet knowing that it's going to be put to bed.

132
00:09:35,101 --> 00:09:39,100
I've enjoyed flying it all these years, and it's a great aircraft.

133
00:09:39,101 --> 00:09:42,100
I'd love to keep flying it until I can't fly anymore.

134
00:09:42,101 --> 00:09:44,100
Unfortunately, that's just the way it is.

135
00:09:44,101 --> 00:09:46,980
Now, this is the second Grumman aircraft you've put to bed.

136
00:09:47,480 --> 00:09:49,560
We want to shake that curse right now.

137
00:09:49,561 --> 00:09:51,000
You may not want to get too close to me.

138
00:09:51,860 --> 00:09:56,220
I used to fly the A-6, and it was tough to get rid of that airplane, too,

139
00:09:56,260 --> 00:09:58,420
but I've really enjoyed flying the Tomcat as well.

140
00:09:58,520 --> 00:10:00,760
It's very bittersweet when you lose.

141
00:10:01,540 --> 00:10:04,300
Say goodbye to one airplane and say hello to a new one.

142
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:08,640
On the flight deck, the plane captains make their final checks

143
00:10:08,641 --> 00:10:12,160
before handing off the last three F-14s to the flight deck controllers.

144
00:10:14,100 --> 00:10:20,100
It's got a lot of history behind it stemming out of Bethpage, New York and the Grumman Aircraft Corporation.

145
00:10:20,101 --> 00:10:23,100
But here's the last aircraft taking the last shot.

146
00:10:24,100 --> 00:10:30,100
With a series of hand signals from the flight crews, two of the three Tomcats roar down the flight deck

147
00:10:30,101 --> 00:10:36,100
and reach a speed of nearly 150 miles an hour in just two seconds.

148
00:10:39,100 --> 00:10:42,100
Now, the final F-14 taxis into position.

149
00:10:42,101 --> 00:10:49,100
Flight controls are checked to ensure they are operating correctly.

150
00:10:49,101 --> 00:10:56,100
Everything looks good, so the tower gives the all clear.

151
00:11:03,100 --> 00:11:10,100
The pilot circles around to make one last fly-by-pass, traveling at supersonic speed.

152
00:11:12,100 --> 00:11:18,660
His way of saying farewell to his shipmates before making the short trip back to Naval

153
00:11:18,661 --> 00:11:22,420
Air Station Oceana in Virginia.

154
00:11:22,421 --> 00:11:36,040
We now return to the special edition of Modern Marvels, the final farewell to the F-14 Tomcat.

155
00:11:36,041 --> 00:11:41,400
Although a new generation of state-of-the-art aircraft now flies in its place, the F-14

156
00:11:41,401 --> 00:11:44,640
always endure as an aerial marvel in a league of its own.

157
00:11:46,640 --> 00:11:50,640
There's nothing like an F-14 when it's doing burner takeoffs and things of that nature.

158
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:53,200
It's just still awesome after all these years.

159
00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:57,780
Just the sheer size of it and the fact that the wings that swept always was something

160
00:11:57,781 --> 00:12:00,660
that always caught the public's eye when they see the F-14.

161
00:12:00,800 --> 00:12:01,800
It's just very recognizable.

162
00:12:03,220 --> 00:12:08,940
There's no other airplane before that or since that has the entire range of capabilities,

163
00:12:08,941 --> 00:12:14,680
carrying all kinds of weapons, doing dogfights, doing maritime air superiority, combat air defense.

164
00:12:15,560 --> 00:12:18,580
And it's done those things extremely well throughout its history

165
00:12:18,581 --> 00:12:22,620
and goes out, I think, as perhaps the most lethal platform on the planet.

166
00:12:26,320 --> 00:12:29,840
The Tomcat's arsenal can include one 20-millimeter cannon,

167
00:12:30,420 --> 00:12:32,460
four Sidewinder air-to-air missiles,

168
00:12:33,520 --> 00:12:35,580
six Sparrow air-to-air missiles,

169
00:12:36,320 --> 00:12:38,480
six 1,000-pound Phoenix missiles,

170
00:12:38,940 --> 00:12:42,540
and four 2,000-pound precision-guided munitions.

171
00:12:44,420 --> 00:12:50,240
You have the ability to cycle between an M61 gun, 20-millimeter cannon.

172
00:12:50,840 --> 00:12:53,700
You can go to a sidewinder, air-to-air, heat-seeking missile.

173
00:12:54,260 --> 00:12:58,340
You can go to a radar sparrow if you wanted an intermediate range.

174
00:12:58,800 --> 00:13:00,400
And you can go with a long-range Phoenix.

175
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:06,240
Beyond its versatile firepower, the F-14 features a radar antenna

176
00:13:06,241 --> 00:13:09,500
with a range longer than that of any fighter jet today.

177
00:13:10,340 --> 00:13:14,220
If you look at some of the other airplanes, fighter airplanes, that are out there these days,

178
00:13:14,520 --> 00:13:16,660
you'll see that the noses are a little bit smaller,

179
00:13:16,980 --> 00:13:19,760
and that means, hey, that their antennas are smaller also.

180
00:13:20,780 --> 00:13:27,360
The F-14's radar can detect targets as low as 50 feet and as high as 80,000 feet.

181
00:13:28,620 --> 00:13:34,060
It can track 24 targets and enable pilots to fire their six Phoenix missiles

182
00:13:34,061 --> 00:13:36,260
at six different targets simultaneously.

183
00:13:38,600 --> 00:13:42,260
Since these missiles can be fired from almost 100 miles away,

184
00:13:43,440 --> 00:13:47,940
the F-14's adversaries at long range have no idea that they are under attack.

185
00:13:50,220 --> 00:13:55,260
It also has defensive electronic countermeasures built into the radar.

186
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:00,820
So it's harder for the enemy to detect and to jam against this radar.

187
00:14:00,821 --> 00:14:05,100
And by the time his radar would have detected you, he'd be dead.

188
00:14:06,720 --> 00:14:14,080
It remains today, really, the benchmark of the multi-mission fighter attack airplane.

189
00:14:14,900 --> 00:14:17,180
It's high-speed intercept, Mach 2.4.

190
00:14:17,300 --> 00:14:20,360
It's capable of air combat maneuvering, variable sweep wing.

191
00:14:20,440 --> 00:14:22,660
Gives it a dogfight capability second to none.

192
00:14:23,940 --> 00:14:29,540
The F-14's variable geometry swept wings shift their position based on the jet's airspeed.

193
00:14:30,820 --> 00:14:37,340
The wings sweep back as far as 68 degrees at supersonic speeds and can

194
00:14:37,341 --> 00:14:41,220
sweep forward to 20 degrees to allow takeoffs and landings on aircraft

195
00:14:41,221 --> 00:14:48,300
carriers. Fully extended, the wings enable as much air as possible to pass

196
00:14:48,301 --> 00:14:54,760
around them, generating optimum length. Swept back at higher speeds, the wings

197
00:14:54,761 --> 00:14:59,820
minimize the F-14's drag.

198
00:14:59,821 --> 00:15:07,720
Another signature feature of the Tomcat provides its prodigious thrust, twin turbojet engines.

199
00:15:07,721 --> 00:15:13,180
There are two series of engines that have been used in the F-14.

200
00:15:13,181 --> 00:15:17,720
The oldest is the TF Pratt & Whitney TF-30 engine.

201
00:15:17,721 --> 00:15:21,380
It had about 17,000 pounds of thrust available.

202
00:15:21,381 --> 00:15:27,540
The follow-on for that engine was the GE 110 that all the Bs and the Ds are flying today.

203
00:15:27,541 --> 00:15:36,480
It has about 10,000 pounds more thrust per engine, so a total complement of about 56,000 pounds of thrust.

204
00:15:38,040 --> 00:15:44,840
The afterburner capability of this GE F 110 engine allows this airplane to accelerate to speeds in excess of Mach 2.3,

205
00:15:45,180 --> 00:15:49,180
which is approximately 1,500 miles per hour if you're looking at your speedometer.

206
00:15:49,181 --> 00:15:56,180
At such lightning speeds, controlling the rush of air entering the jet engine intakes is imperative.

207
00:15:56,181 --> 00:16:00,180
This is one of the intakes for the F-14B that we're looking at here.

208
00:16:00,181 --> 00:16:05,180
Since this is a supersonic fighter, we have to kind of slow down the air before it hits the motor.

209
00:16:05,181 --> 00:16:08,180
If supersonic air does hit the motor, it'll stall it.

210
00:16:08,181 --> 00:16:12,180
So when they developed the F-14, they developed an air inlet control system,

211
00:16:12,181 --> 00:16:17,180
which allows these ramps, there's actually three ramps, number one, number two, and number three ramps,

212
00:16:17,181 --> 00:16:20,180
These program down as a function of your Mach number.

213
00:16:20,181 --> 00:16:24,180
And the faster you go as you approach supersonic speed, Mach 1,

214
00:16:24,181 --> 00:16:28,180
all these ramps will be deflected down, decreasing the error that goes in here.

215
00:16:28,181 --> 00:16:31,180
So it controls the amount of flow that goes into the motor.

216
00:16:31,181 --> 00:16:38,180
The F-14's superb aerodynamics are matched only by its prowess as a combat fighter.

217
00:16:40,180 --> 00:16:43,180
To hunt ground targets, it relies on the Lantern,

218
00:16:43,181 --> 00:16:48,180
short for Low Altitude, Navigation and Targeting Infrared for Night.

219
00:16:49,180 --> 00:16:55,180
Its infrared imaging system can identify targets from altitudes of 40,000 feet.

220
00:16:56,180 --> 00:17:03,180
Then the pod's laser can fire a beam to the target to precisely guide the F-14's 2,000-pound smart bombs.

221
00:17:05,180 --> 00:17:10,180
A separate piece of hardware helps the F-14's pilots hone in on air-to-air targets.

222
00:17:10,181 --> 00:17:11,180
air targets.

223
00:17:11,181 --> 00:17:17,360
This is the TCS camera system, television camera set, and the unique thing about this

224
00:17:17,361 --> 00:17:22,520
with the F-14 is it allows me to slew this to where the radar is pointing and I can actually

225
00:17:22,521 --> 00:17:26,820
get a contrast lock on an unidentified aircraft out there.

226
00:17:26,821 --> 00:17:30,980
Take a look and put it on my display and it allows me to identify an airplane before I

227
00:17:30,981 --> 00:17:34,400
can see it within the visual range.

228
00:17:34,401 --> 00:17:39,280
In other words, this video system acts as the pilot's long-range eyes, helping him

229
00:17:39,281 --> 00:17:43,920
distinguished between friend and foe in the sky.

230
00:17:43,921 --> 00:17:49,600
The Tomcat can come equipped with yet another targeting device called TARSE.

231
00:17:49,601 --> 00:17:54,820
Short for Tactical Airborne Reconnaissance Pod System, it provides clear digital still

232
00:17:54,821 --> 00:17:58,280
images of targets up to 600 miles away.

233
00:17:58,281 --> 00:18:07,460
Go in and fly for more than a thousand miles, go in deep into enemy territory, get pictures,

234
00:18:07,461 --> 00:18:12,780
photographs. Such impressive features embodied in such a nimble and deadly

235
00:18:12,781 --> 00:18:19,160
aircraft have combined to grant the Tomcat a mystique all its own. The origins

236
00:18:19,161 --> 00:18:24,260
of the F-14 date back to the early 1960s when America's Cold War with the Soviet

237
00:18:24,261 --> 00:18:30,560
Union was heating up. The Russians were developing a large family of surface-to-

238
00:18:30,561 --> 00:18:36,680
surface cruise missiles that flew extremely low. At such low altitudes

239
00:18:36,681 --> 00:18:40,380
there were more signals that could interfere with the radar on U.S. ships,

240
00:18:40,580 --> 00:18:44,500
which might be unable to detect these missiles until they were too close.

241
00:18:47,440 --> 00:18:50,820
Soviets also possessed deadly air-to-surface bombs.

242
00:18:52,480 --> 00:18:55,940
U.S. fighter planes did not have the ability to intercept them.

243
00:18:56,400 --> 00:19:00,300
The Russian attack aircraft that could come in from, you know, hundreds of miles away

244
00:19:00,301 --> 00:19:05,280
and do a pretty devastating job in a carrier group if they got too close.

245
00:19:05,281 --> 00:19:10,280
The Pentagon realized it needed an aircraft that could take on the Soviets.

246
00:19:10,281 --> 00:19:17,280
We needed air superiority, which required an air combat maneuverable fighter aircraft,

247
00:19:17,281 --> 00:19:21,280
capable of being nimble and agile and dogfighting.

248
00:19:21,281 --> 00:19:27,280
The plane also needed to carry a powerful radar system to help it quickly intercept an enemy's missile.

249
00:19:27,281 --> 00:19:33,280
And that was the ability to protect the carrier fleet and to be able to go out hundreds of miles

250
00:19:33,281 --> 00:19:38,080
miles and detect anybody that's incoming from a long range.

251
00:19:38,081 --> 00:19:44,460
In November 1962, the Pentagon awarded the Grumman Corporation a contract to build an

252
00:19:44,461 --> 00:19:51,840
interceptor version of a fighter already in development, the General Dynamics F-111.

253
00:19:51,841 --> 00:19:59,700
Grumman's F-111 for the Navy began flying on May 18, 1965, but it had several problems.

254
00:19:59,701 --> 00:20:02,620
was a whopping 85,000-pound fighter.

255
00:20:03,100 --> 00:20:05,240
Too difficult to land on the aircraft carrier,

256
00:20:05,560 --> 00:20:09,060
and its swing wings had to be operated manually by the pilot.

257
00:20:09,840 --> 00:20:12,060
What the Navy really wanted was a fighter aircraft,

258
00:20:12,380 --> 00:20:15,800
and the fighter aircraft that the F-111 represented

259
00:20:15,801 --> 00:20:17,080
wasn't a particularly good fighter.

260
00:20:17,580 --> 00:20:18,420
It was too big.

261
00:20:19,020 --> 00:20:20,480
It carried too much fuel.

262
00:20:21,240 --> 00:20:24,660
By 1966, the problematic F-111

263
00:20:24,661 --> 00:20:27,120
had become a time-consuming headache for the Navy,

264
00:20:27,121 --> 00:20:33,040
prompting its leaders to create a new jet fighter with a demanding set of requirements.

265
00:20:35,060 --> 00:20:42,340
Mach 2 speed, great maneuverability, a powerful radar, and the ability to carry a variety of weapons.

266
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:49,060
Furthermore, the new plane had to be light enough to take off from and land on an aircraft carrier.

267
00:20:49,061 --> 00:20:56,060
During the summer of 1968, the Navy solicited engineering proposals for the new fighter

268
00:20:56,061 --> 00:20:59,060
from five leading aerospace contractors.

269
00:20:59,061 --> 00:21:04,060
In January of 1969, Grumman again was declared the winner.

270
00:21:04,061 --> 00:21:09,060
Its $5 billion military contract was the largest in history.

271
00:21:09,061 --> 00:21:12,060
But the contract was demanding.

272
00:21:12,061 --> 00:21:17,060
Grumman was bound to deliver 700 airplanes over eight years.

273
00:21:17,061 --> 00:21:23,060
Even more daunting, it would need to have a plane ready for test flight in 17 months,

274
00:21:23,061 --> 00:21:26,060
half the usual time for a program of this type.

275
00:21:28,060 --> 00:21:34,060
From a contract signature to the delivery of the airplane, we had a requirement of 51 months.

276
00:21:34,061 --> 00:21:38,060
Work on the plane began in August 1969.

277
00:21:39,060 --> 00:21:46,060
One of Grumman's first decisions was to include a feature originally designed for the Air Force's YF-12 Blackbird.

278
00:21:47,060 --> 00:21:52,060
and adopted by the F-111B, the AUG-9 Doppler radar system.

279
00:21:53,560 --> 00:21:57,060
This was a significant improvement over pulse radar from World War II,

280
00:21:57,560 --> 00:22:00,060
which was still used on aircraft carriers in the 1960s.

281
00:22:02,560 --> 00:22:05,060
Pulse radar measures distances from targets.

282
00:22:05,560 --> 00:22:08,060
It works effectively in the open sea.

283
00:22:08,560 --> 00:22:11,060
But closer to land, stationary features, like mountains,

284
00:22:11,560 --> 00:22:14,060
clutter the radar operator's display.

285
00:22:14,061 --> 00:22:20,260
Doppler radar only measures moving targets without regard to background.

286
00:22:20,261 --> 00:22:25,060
It could easily detect Soviet cruise missiles.

287
00:22:25,061 --> 00:22:30,520
While the F-111 had only two forms of pulse radar and two forms of Doppler, the new plane

288
00:22:30,521 --> 00:22:37,460
would have six radar modes, four in Doppler and two in pulse.

289
00:22:37,461 --> 00:22:42,940
This doubled the number of targets the jet could track, from 12 to 24.

290
00:22:42,941 --> 00:22:46,100
they could counter the crew's missiles from greater distances.

291
00:22:47,340 --> 00:22:49,200
With a proper air-to-air weapon,

292
00:22:49,201 --> 00:22:52,180
the jet could theoretically down the Soviet missiles.

293
00:22:53,180 --> 00:22:57,880
The AUG-9 was an excellent transfer to the F-14.

294
00:22:57,881 --> 00:23:01,180
We needed it for the excellent range it has

295
00:23:01,181 --> 00:23:02,940
and for its excellent capability.

296
00:23:02,941 --> 00:23:05,020
Developing a sophisticated radar system

297
00:23:05,021 --> 00:23:07,480
for the new jet fighter was one thing.

298
00:23:07,481 --> 00:23:10,800
Designing the new state-of-the-art fighter itself was another.

299
00:23:10,801 --> 00:23:16,480
To answer the challenge, Grumman engineers would have to reinvent the basic components

300
00:23:16,481 --> 00:23:21,000
of the military aircraft.

301
00:23:21,001 --> 00:23:26,980
The name Tomcat was selected partly as a tribute to the F-14 program's strongest supporters,

302
00:23:26,981 --> 00:23:30,360
Navy Admirals Thomas Connolly and Thomas Maurer.

303
00:23:30,361 --> 00:23:38,900
We'll be right back with more modern marvels here on the History Channel.

304
00:23:38,901 --> 00:23:45,140
In 1969, after landing the $5 billion contract for the F-14, the Grumman Corporation set

305
00:23:45,141 --> 00:23:48,740
its sights on the new fighter plane's design.

306
00:23:48,741 --> 00:23:55,780
Engineers faced the sobering challenge of designing a jet plane that had to travel at Mach 2 speeds

307
00:23:55,781 --> 00:24:00,720
and maneuver spryly in battle while carrying tons of weapons.

308
00:24:00,721 --> 00:24:03,720
Grumman's first priority was the swing wing.

309
00:24:03,721 --> 00:24:05,220
The concept is simple.

310
00:24:05,221 --> 00:24:11,840
A swing wing enables a plane to fly faster by reducing the wing's frontal area and the plane's overall drag.

311
00:24:13,500 --> 00:24:20,300
In May 1952, Grumman had developed the first Navy swing wing plane to fly, the XF-10.

312
00:24:21,040 --> 00:24:26,100
But it was plagued by a variety of engine, stability, and control problems.

313
00:24:28,100 --> 00:24:31,680
In 1953, the XF-10 program was scrapped.

314
00:24:32,360 --> 00:24:34,960
But the swing wing concept had shown great promise.

315
00:24:35,220 --> 00:24:38,120
We learned an awful lot in the process of doing it.

316
00:24:39,120 --> 00:24:47,360
And after that, we were teamed with General Dynamics in the production of the F-111 aircraft.

317
00:24:47,940 --> 00:24:54,100
There was another case where Grumman got very valuable experience in dealing with variable sweep wings.

318
00:24:54,101 --> 00:25:00,800
We pretty well had a grip on what not to do when the F-14 program came along.

319
00:25:01,360 --> 00:25:02,240
But not at first.

320
00:25:02,241 --> 00:25:06,480
designer Bob Kress and some of his colleagues disagreed about the airspeed

321
00:25:06,481 --> 00:25:09,620
at which the F-14's wings should be swept back.

322
00:25:09,621 --> 00:25:15,980
Kress' fellow designers felt that speed should be Mach 1.2 or 900 miles per hour.

323
00:25:15,981 --> 00:25:21,620
When the fellows came in and dumped that number on me, I was quite shocked.

324
00:25:21,621 --> 00:25:26,360
And I started to ask, what would anybody be doing at Mach 1.2?

325
00:25:26,361 --> 00:25:30,140
With the wings unswept, the airplane would be screaming at them.

326
00:25:32,240 --> 00:25:39,240
Before the F-14, pilots flying swing-wing aircraft were the ones responsible for sweeping the wings back.

327
00:25:39,241 --> 00:25:46,240
Kress felt a system was needed that would relieve pilots of concerns about the swing-wing's optimum position.

328
00:25:46,241 --> 00:25:51,240
His team devised an air data computer called the Mach Sweep Programmer.

329
00:25:51,241 --> 00:25:56,240
It would automatically sweep the wings without any input from the pilot.

330
00:25:56,241 --> 00:26:00,740
pilot. That's probably the most unique characteristic of the airplane in the

331
00:26:00,741 --> 00:26:06,080
sense that it provided the first truly computer-controlled operational variable

332
00:26:06,081 --> 00:26:09,680
swept-wing aircraft. The pilot could override the computer if it was a

333
00:26:09,681 --> 00:26:15,980
computer failure. Kress's team subjected the wings to countless wind tunnel trials.

334
00:26:15,981 --> 00:26:24,040
What we did as the Mach number increased beyond Mach 0.7, we gradually started to

335
00:26:24,041 --> 00:26:29,800
sweep the wings back until they were at full sweep at about Mach 0.95.

336
00:26:29,801 --> 00:26:36,040
There was tremendous compatibility between limitation of bending moments and making the

337
00:26:36,041 --> 00:26:42,860
airplane a superb fighter in terms of drag and buffet and turning performance.

338
00:26:42,861 --> 00:26:47,120
Weight considerations were a key factor in choosing what material would be used to construct

339
00:26:47,121 --> 00:26:50,160
the swing wings.

340
00:26:50,161 --> 00:26:57,160
Since the F-14 would carry tons of weapons, Grumman chose titanium, which is both lighter and stronger than steel.

341
00:27:00,160 --> 00:27:08,160
Welders fused several pieces of titanium together to form a key part of the F-14's airframe, the wing box.

342
00:27:08,161 --> 00:27:15,876
The wing box structure is the structure that effectively went from one side of the aircraft on its wing pivot to the other side of the aircraft on that side's wing

343
00:27:15,877 --> 00:27:16,577
pivot.

344
00:27:16,578 --> 00:27:20,560
The titanium box beam was a marvelous piece of engineering.

345
00:27:20,561 --> 00:27:25,760
It weighs 2,500 pounds and it's all welded together.

346
00:27:25,761 --> 00:27:28,760
It holds the whole airplane together.

347
00:27:29,760 --> 00:27:36,560
The completed wing box and airframe showcased their structural integrity during more than 500 test drops.

348
00:27:37,560 --> 00:27:41,160
The F-14 would be made up of 35% titanium.

349
00:27:41,960 --> 00:27:45,560
While its wing box was composed entirely of titanium,

350
00:27:45,561 --> 00:27:50,440
The rest of the aircraft would consist of approximately 40% aluminum alloy,

351
00:27:51,180 --> 00:27:55,180
less than 1% boron, and about 18% steel.

352
00:27:56,260 --> 00:28:01,840
The F-14's high percentage of titanium helped make it 40,000 pounds lighter than the F-111.

353
00:28:03,260 --> 00:28:07,280
Its trim physique would enable it to increase its thrust-to-weight ratio,

354
00:28:07,281 --> 00:28:10,520
a crucial measure of aerodynamic performance.

355
00:28:10,521 --> 00:28:19,520
The ratio was calculated by dividing an aircraft's thrust, measured in pounds, by the aircraft's weight in pounds.

356
00:28:19,521 --> 00:28:23,520
The greater the ratio, the better the performance.

357
00:28:23,521 --> 00:28:30,520
In order to achieve the ideal thrust-to-weight ratio, Grumman engineers began an exhaustive series of tests,

358
00:28:30,521 --> 00:28:33,520
with most of the emphasis on the swing wing.

359
00:28:33,521 --> 00:28:40,180
They subjected models in more than 2,000 configurations to over 9,000 hours of wind tunnel testing.

360
00:28:41,040 --> 00:28:45,420
By January of 1968, they'd found their ideal model.

361
00:28:46,380 --> 00:28:54,240
Number 303E, a swing-wing craft powered by twin jet engines, each producing 20,000 pounds of thrust.

362
00:28:55,080 --> 00:28:59,620
The twin engines were significantly separated from one another.

363
00:28:59,621 --> 00:29:07,240
and early on we didn't really realize how valuable that separation would become

364
00:29:07,241 --> 00:29:11,820
as a place where we could nest missiles one behind the other.

365
00:29:12,360 --> 00:29:17,000
Missiles as big as the Phoenix missile and as big as 2,000 pound bombs.

366
00:29:18,620 --> 00:29:24,400
The F-111 had an internal bomb bay and permanent fittings for carrying the six Phoenix missiles.

367
00:29:26,480 --> 00:29:29,080
The Tomcat carried its weapons externally.

368
00:29:29,620 --> 00:29:32,620
It's what we call the pallet concept.

369
00:29:32,621 --> 00:29:35,620
And if you look at the bottom of the F-14 airplane,

370
00:29:35,621 --> 00:29:38,620
you'll see the two Phoenix missiles,

371
00:29:38,621 --> 00:29:40,620
or two 2,000-pound bombs.

372
00:29:40,621 --> 00:29:42,620
They're located on rails,

373
00:29:42,621 --> 00:29:46,620
and the rails are contoured such that the fins of the missiles

374
00:29:46,621 --> 00:29:50,620
and part of the missile is covered by the rails,

375
00:29:50,621 --> 00:29:54,620
which cuts the aerodynamic drag of the weapons.

376
00:29:54,621 --> 00:29:57,620
Weapons crews could change the pallets quickly,

377
00:29:57,621 --> 00:30:01,300
quickly, depending on the mission.

378
00:30:01,301 --> 00:30:03,280
Another innovation by Grumman engineers

379
00:30:03,281 --> 00:30:06,840
were the F-14's twin vertical tail design.

380
00:30:06,841 --> 00:30:10,700
This configuration had never been used before in any jet fighter.

381
00:30:10,701 --> 00:30:14,120
Two shorter tails, as opposed to a single taller tail,

382
00:30:14,121 --> 00:30:16,580
would make it easier for pilots to maneuver the plane

383
00:30:16,581 --> 00:30:20,000
on carrier hangar decks with height limits.

384
00:30:20,001 --> 00:30:21,980
But most importantly, the two tails

385
00:30:21,981 --> 00:30:25,060
would create greater directional stability.

386
00:30:25,061 --> 00:30:32,280
When you have two tails, you have twice as much surface that can be applied to the aerodynamic forces.

387
00:30:32,600 --> 00:30:34,920
You can get twice as much correction factors.

388
00:30:35,040 --> 00:30:36,960
You want something that straightens the airplane out.

389
00:30:37,020 --> 00:30:40,300
If you lose an engine, it tends to slide laterally and rotate.

390
00:30:40,780 --> 00:30:42,100
You want it to stop rotating.

391
00:30:43,260 --> 00:30:48,060
Another revolutionary design concept came from Bob Kress, the crew of two.

392
00:30:48,800 --> 00:30:53,580
The pilot and the REO, or radar intercept officer, would be seated in tandem.

393
00:30:53,581 --> 00:30:58,580
This configuration would reduce the plane's drag because it would make for a thinner cockpit.

394
00:30:58,581 --> 00:31:04,580
The target date for the test flight of the first 12 prototypes was January 1971.

395
00:31:04,581 --> 00:31:10,580
Grumman planned to get its first innovative bird in the air a month ahead of schedule.

396
00:31:10,581 --> 00:31:18,580
Confidence was running high, but looming ahead were unexpected dangers and sudden death.

397
00:31:18,581 --> 00:31:22,460
sudden death.

398
00:31:22,461 --> 00:31:27,040
The Grumman Corporation faced substantial contractual penalties if the jet it produced

399
00:31:27,041 --> 00:31:29,520
failed to meet the Navy's specifications.

400
00:31:29,521 --> 00:31:36,040
For example, each 100 pounds of excess weight would draw a fine of $440,000.

401
00:31:36,041 --> 00:31:40,960
We'll be right back with more Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

402
00:31:40,961 --> 00:31:48,180
We now return to this special edition of Modern Marvels, the final farewell to the F-14 Tomcat.

403
00:31:48,181 --> 00:31:55,560
The first of what would be thousands of test flights for the new F-14 was planned for December

404
00:31:55,561 --> 00:32:00,040
21, 1970, a month ahead of schedule.

405
00:32:00,041 --> 00:32:04,620
The location, Grumman's Test Flight Center in Calverton, New York.

406
00:32:04,621 --> 00:32:08,940
Robert Smyth and Bill Miller were the first test crew.

407
00:32:08,941 --> 00:32:14,300
As darkness fell, the prototype made two wide circles around the airfield, completing a

408
00:32:14,301 --> 00:32:17,500
simple but successful ten-minute flight.

409
00:32:17,501 --> 00:32:22,500
Test flight number two came nine days later on December 30th.

410
00:32:22,501 --> 00:32:28,500
This time the plane was to make its first efforts to go supersonic and sweep the wings.

411
00:32:28,501 --> 00:32:32,500
Smythe and Miller would again be in the cockpit.

412
00:32:32,501 --> 00:32:36,500
Flight test engineer Dennis Romano followed in a chase plane.

413
00:32:36,501 --> 00:32:39,500
It was a lot of hoopla and there was a lot of people there.

414
00:32:39,501 --> 00:32:45,500
F-14 got airborne and flew out off the south shore of Long Island.

415
00:32:45,501 --> 00:32:48,620
and there was a good stiff breeze blowing probably about 25 knots.

416
00:32:49,980 --> 00:32:54,600
The flight began smoothly, but miles away from the base at 10,000 feet,

417
00:32:55,660 --> 00:32:57,820
the F-14 started leaking hydraulic fluid.

418
00:33:00,420 --> 00:33:02,480
The leak was caused by a resonance,

419
00:33:03,060 --> 00:33:06,680
the rhythmic shaking produced by pulsations from the hydraulic pumps.

420
00:33:06,980 --> 00:33:09,100
This led to a break in the hydraulic lines.

421
00:33:10,040 --> 00:33:12,080
The pilots were forced to bring the plane back.

422
00:33:12,081 --> 00:33:21,900
We lost the hydraulic power for the controls, so we tried to get it to field, and he almost got back, and all of a sudden the airplane started to fishtail.

423
00:33:22,940 --> 00:33:28,000
And they just speared in a plume of smoke because they were going down with the airplane.

424
00:33:30,080 --> 00:33:32,080
And I felt I had lost two friends.

425
00:33:33,200 --> 00:33:37,009
I saw the backseat guy, Bob Smythe, and the frontseat guy, Bill Miller, come out.

426
00:33:37,010 --> 00:33:37,710
They ejected.

427
00:33:37,711 --> 00:33:41,620
I saw Bob Smythe's parachute descending into the exploding fireball.

428
00:33:41,860 --> 00:33:45,680
Heat from the rising fireball lifted him up, and the wind blew him after the wreckage.

429
00:33:46,080 --> 00:33:48,740
And the bottom of his flight suit was singed from the heat.

430
00:33:49,620 --> 00:33:50,600
But they both escaped.

431
00:33:51,200 --> 00:33:53,740
The aircraft, unfortunately, was completely lost.

432
00:33:54,180 --> 00:33:58,320
And all it took was to go ahead and change some of the clamping on the hydraulic,

433
00:33:58,600 --> 00:33:59,680
and the problem went away.

434
00:34:00,820 --> 00:34:04,360
Five months later, the same test pilots, Miller and Smythe,

435
00:34:04,361 --> 00:34:10,540
successfully reached supersonic speed, and deployed the swing wings for the first time.

436
00:34:11,380 --> 00:34:18,020
In the months to come, Grumman continued its trials, conducting 600 separate tests with each flight.

437
00:34:19,440 --> 00:34:22,520
Sensors transmitted information to engineers manning computers.

438
00:34:23,420 --> 00:34:26,780
They would examine the data, then clear the pilots for the next test flight.

439
00:34:26,781 --> 00:34:36,660
Let's say out on a wing panel you had 50 stress points on the wing that you wanted to measure.

440
00:34:36,661 --> 00:34:40,620
Each wing you put 50 instrumentation points.

441
00:34:40,621 --> 00:34:44,020
These are little sensors and each one of those is a parameter.

442
00:34:44,021 --> 00:34:47,820
600 of those are being recorded constantly.

443
00:34:47,821 --> 00:34:52,280
Some of them are electrical, some of them are stress, structure, engine, fuel system.

444
00:34:52,281 --> 00:34:57,520
And what you happen to be testing primarily that day is where the focus and the emphasis is.

445
00:34:57,521 --> 00:35:04,160
We could go out and continue to do our testing until the engineer on his flight test console

446
00:35:04,161 --> 00:35:10,100
would see some sort of a danger point and decide at least we would stop that portion of the test

447
00:35:10,101 --> 00:35:12,040
but then go on to some others.

448
00:35:13,900 --> 00:35:18,440
The structural test program was the most demanding phase for both plane and pilot.

449
00:35:18,441 --> 00:35:25,440
The F-14 flew every maneuver imaginable at low speeds, high speeds, and at all altitudes.

450
00:35:25,441 --> 00:35:30,540
Sometimes, depending upon the flight configuration and the loading of the airplane, we would

451
00:35:30,541 --> 00:35:35,200
roll the airplane as high as 270 degrees per second.

452
00:35:35,201 --> 00:35:39,440
For an airplane that weighed 40,000 pounds, that was pretty good.

453
00:35:39,441 --> 00:35:40,440
A lot of inertia.

454
00:35:40,441 --> 00:35:42,440
We did that at twice the speed of sound.

455
00:35:42,441 --> 00:35:45,440
Then we also did it at 900 miles an hour at 5,000 feet.

456
00:35:45,441 --> 00:35:50,440
Up to 135 structural test points were assessed in a four-hour flight.

457
00:35:50,441 --> 00:35:55,440
And I will tell you that even for a young man at the end of a four-and-a-half-hour structural flight,

458
00:35:55,441 --> 00:35:57,440
you were pretty worn out.

459
00:35:57,441 --> 00:36:00,440
The rigorous test flights finally exacted a terrible price.

460
00:36:00,441 --> 00:36:06,440
In June 1971, Bill Miller, who piloted the first flights,

461
00:36:06,441 --> 00:36:10,440
mysteriously lost control of his plane and died in a crash.

462
00:36:10,441 --> 00:36:16,800
But the testing continued.

463
00:36:16,801 --> 00:36:23,020
The most vexing problem proved to be the F-14's engine, the same basic model that Grumman

464
00:36:23,021 --> 00:36:27,020
had been contractually bound to use for the F-111.

465
00:36:27,021 --> 00:36:32,280
Grumman had installed Pratt & Whitney's TF-30 engine on most of its tests and all of its

466
00:36:32,281 --> 00:36:34,740
production F-14s.

467
00:36:34,741 --> 00:36:41,740
The engine had difficulty coping with both extreme aerodynamic maneuvering and high load stresses.

468
00:36:42,740 --> 00:36:45,740
Every time you fly, you're stretching the envelope.

469
00:36:45,741 --> 00:36:49,740
You're not just flying straight and level like a regular jet passenger airplane.

470
00:36:49,741 --> 00:36:51,628
You're flying very fast.

471
00:36:51,629 --> 00:36:56,740
You're stressing it aerodynamically, inertially, every which way.

472
00:36:57,740 --> 00:37:03,740
Sometimes fan blades broke off and flew through the rest of the engine, destroying it entirely.

473
00:37:04,740 --> 00:37:08,140
Engineers solved the problem by developing engine improvements,

474
00:37:08,141 --> 00:37:11,780
including stronger titanium alloy fan blades.

475
00:37:11,781 --> 00:37:15,260
They also added steel containment fans to the fan cases

476
00:37:15,261 --> 00:37:18,580
to prevent damage to the aircraft during possible engine failure.

477
00:37:21,100 --> 00:37:23,220
Additional improvements, like this one,

478
00:37:23,221 --> 00:37:26,660
enabled the F-14 to live up to its potential.

479
00:37:26,661 --> 00:37:30,600
Over the next 18 months, only two more planes were lost.

480
00:37:30,601 --> 00:37:34,200
But the most crucial and intricate test was yet to come,

481
00:37:34,201 --> 00:37:39,560
one that required the plane to fire six Phoenix missiles simultaneously at six separate targets.

482
00:37:40,180 --> 00:37:44,060
You have to have six separate drones flying in six different places,

483
00:37:44,640 --> 00:37:47,280
and you have to have an F-14 with six Phoenix missiles,

484
00:37:47,840 --> 00:37:51,480
and you have to have all the range and all the coordination capability.

485
00:37:51,820 --> 00:37:52,520
A big deal.

486
00:37:52,521 --> 00:37:57,600
Nobody has ever attempted to fire at six widely separated targets simultaneously.

487
00:37:58,460 --> 00:38:03,560
The pioneering test would take place at the Naval Missile Test Center at Point Magoo, California,

488
00:38:03,561 --> 00:38:05,560
in November 1972.

489
00:38:05,561 --> 00:38:07,560
Zero.

490
00:38:07,561 --> 00:38:14,560
The F-14 would fire missiles at targets flying at altitudes ranging from sea level to 100,000 feet.

491
00:38:14,561 --> 00:38:24,560
Clock's one. One's away. Two's in the air. Six in the air.

492
00:38:24,561 --> 00:38:29,560
Only one of the six missiles failed to hit its target.

493
00:38:29,561 --> 00:38:33,000
It hit five directly, smashed right into them.

494
00:38:33,760 --> 00:38:34,460
It's incredible.

495
00:38:34,540 --> 00:38:39,720
And the one that missed, I don't recall exactly if it was within lethal range, but it wasn't far off.

496
00:38:40,440 --> 00:38:42,040
The Navy was sold.

497
00:38:42,820 --> 00:38:47,740
Three months after the six-on-six test, it took delivery of its first F-14s.

498
00:38:48,640 --> 00:38:50,520
The Tomcat program hit stride.

499
00:38:52,560 --> 00:38:56,360
Over the next three decades, thanks to ongoing improvements,

500
00:38:56,361 --> 00:38:59,860
the F-14 would repeatedly prove its worth in combat.

501
00:39:00,780 --> 00:39:05,280
But in 1979, America's attempt to help an ally

502
00:39:05,281 --> 00:39:09,360
would spell near disaster for the Tomcat and the United States.

503
00:39:11,600 --> 00:39:18,240
Over the course of 36 years, a total of 69 aircrew have lost their lives operating the F-14 Tomcat.

504
00:39:18,980 --> 00:39:23,160
We'll be right back with more modern marvels here on the History Channel.

505
00:39:26,360 --> 00:39:35,360
Shortly after its delivery to the Navy in 1973, the F-14 became one of the most respected fighters in the sky.

506
00:39:36,360 --> 00:39:44,360
But the same year, Soviet aggression prompted an unusual decision involving the Tomcat that would put America in a very vulnerable position.

507
00:39:45,360 --> 00:39:52,360
Iran, then America's ally, was concerned about the Soviet MiGs that were entering its airspace at 50,000 feet.

508
00:39:52,361 --> 00:39:57,131
feet. MiGs were able to fly at speeds exceeding Mach 2.

509
00:39:57,132 --> 00:40:00,600
The Shah of Iran asked President Richard

510
00:40:00,601 --> 00:40:05,595
Nixon for help, intercepting the supersonic intruders.

511
00:40:05,596 --> 00:40:08,740
Nixon told the Shah he could order

512
00:40:08,741 --> 00:40:13,900
either the F-14 Tomcat from the U.S. Navy or the F-15 Eagle from the Air Force.

513
00:40:14,700 --> 00:40:20,880
The only airplane that was really capable of taking down the MiG-25 was the F-14 with the

514
00:40:20,881 --> 00:40:22,680
Phoenix missile on the Phoenix missile system.

515
00:40:23,200 --> 00:40:24,860
The F-15 was appealing

516
00:40:24,861 --> 00:40:26,940
to the Shah because it was an Air Force aircraft

517
00:40:26,941 --> 00:40:28,820
and there were a

518
00:40:28,821 --> 00:40:30,620
substantial Air Force presence in Iran.

519
00:40:31,680 --> 00:40:32,840
The Shah asked to make his

520
00:40:32,841 --> 00:40:34,700
decision based on a fly-off or

521
00:40:34,701 --> 00:40:36,540
air show featuring the two planes.

522
00:40:38,080 --> 00:40:38,840
Nixon agreed.

523
00:40:40,040 --> 00:40:40,820
The fly-off

524
00:40:40,821 --> 00:40:43,000
occurred at Andrews Air Force Base in Maryland.

525
00:40:44,960 --> 00:40:46,980
The F-15 flew first.

526
00:40:49,180 --> 00:40:50,740
Then the Tomcat took to

527
00:40:50,741 --> 00:40:55,740
Dennis Romano was aboard as the radar intercept officer.

528
00:40:55,741 --> 00:41:07,740
90 degree turn to the right, a 270 degree turn back to the runway heading, flew down the runway at 500 feet in a 90 degree bank.

529
00:41:11,740 --> 00:41:16,740
And swept the wings forward from 68 degrees to 20 degrees while we were doing that maneuver.

530
00:41:16,741 --> 00:41:24,660
We touched the airplane down and pulled the airplane up into a vertical climb, left the

531
00:41:24,661 --> 00:41:29,820
gear and flaps down, rolled the airplane directly over the end of the runway, which was very

532
00:41:29,821 --> 00:41:30,820
impressive.

533
00:41:30,821 --> 00:41:36,640
Pasha chose the Tomcat, in part because the F-15 had been experiencing ongoing engine

534
00:41:36,641 --> 00:41:39,080
problems.

535
00:41:39,081 --> 00:41:46,820
In January 1976, the Navy delivered the last of 79 F-14s to Iran.

536
00:41:46,821 --> 00:41:52,640
With the exception of a few classified avionics features, Iranian Tomcat was identical to

537
00:41:52,641 --> 00:41:55,720
the U.S. Navy's.

538
00:41:55,721 --> 00:41:59,960
Grumman technicians and pilots provided training and technical support.

539
00:41:59,961 --> 00:42:07,940
Then, in early 1979, America's relations with Iran suddenly disintegrated.

540
00:42:07,941 --> 00:42:12,280
Increasing unrest in Iran led to the overthrow of the Shah.

541
00:42:12,281 --> 00:42:15,380
Now in power was the radical Islamic government

542
00:42:15,381 --> 00:42:16,480
of the Ayatollah Khomeini.

543
00:42:19,440 --> 00:42:22,300
79 of America's elite fighter jets

544
00:42:22,301 --> 00:42:23,780
were in the hands of an enemy.

545
00:42:26,760 --> 00:42:29,660
Then came a new twist to the story.

546
00:42:29,661 --> 00:42:32,100
By August, the Iranians had discovered

547
00:42:32,101 --> 00:42:34,000
that every Tomcat in their arsenal

548
00:42:34,001 --> 00:42:36,860
was incapable of firing its missiles.

549
00:42:36,861 --> 00:42:39,620
What happened remains unclear to this day,

550
00:42:39,621 --> 00:42:45,220
but some suspect that the Grumman technicians sabotaged the F-14s before they evacuated.

551
00:42:45,221 --> 00:42:49,540
The rumor that I heard was that they took some of the electronics as they were leaving.

552
00:42:49,541 --> 00:42:53,480
I do not know whether that's a fact or not.

553
00:42:53,481 --> 00:42:55,460
What is known is this.

554
00:42:55,461 --> 00:42:58,654
An arms embargo imposed against Iran by the U.S.

555
00:42:58,655 --> 00:43:02,180
crippled Iran's efforts to supply parts for the F-14s

556
00:43:02,181 --> 00:43:06,700
and to provide necessary maintenance.

557
00:43:06,701 --> 00:43:12,060
They're very detailed inspections and parts that have to be replaced on a regular basis.

558
00:43:12,880 --> 00:43:17,120
And if you don't maintain it, in a couple of months, it'll just go into complete disrepair.

559
00:43:17,200 --> 00:43:18,300
And I think that's what really happened.

560
00:43:18,980 --> 00:43:25,360
The real knowledge base was the group of technicians that had gone to support the F-14.

561
00:43:25,540 --> 00:43:29,860
Once they lost the parts and once they lost the expertise, it's just a matter of time.

562
00:43:29,861 --> 00:43:37,900
As far as we know, the airplanes are essentially sitting in desert storage with very little usage and no spare parts.

563
00:43:39,020 --> 00:43:45,560
In 1981, two years after the intrigue in Iran, the Tomcat exhibited its prowess.

564
00:43:46,340 --> 00:43:50,740
It began when the USS Nimitz entered the Gulf of Sidra in the southern Mediterranean,

565
00:43:51,440 --> 00:43:55,280
which Libya had claimed as its own territorial waters.

566
00:43:55,281 --> 00:44:03,280
The Nimitz's purpose was to demonstrate right of navigation in defiance of Libya's so-called line of death.

567
00:44:03,281 --> 00:44:10,280
The Nimitz Tomcats came under fire by Soviet Su-22s flown by Libyan pilots.

568
00:44:10,281 --> 00:44:15,280
Using sidewinders, the Tomcats shot down both Soviet jets.

569
00:44:15,281 --> 00:44:21,280
Eight years later, Libyan pilots again confronted the F-14.

570
00:44:21,281 --> 00:44:22,280
the F-14.

571
00:44:22,281 --> 00:44:25,660
Toes out 53 miles now.

572
00:44:25,661 --> 00:44:29,520
This time, they were flying the swift and mighty Soviet MiG fighters.

573
00:44:29,521 --> 00:44:36,520
Second engagement, we actually tried four or five different maneuvers to avoid the airplanes

574
00:44:36,521 --> 00:44:37,520
that were oncoming.

575
00:44:37,521 --> 00:44:42,780
And each time we made a turn, they turned into us, which meant that it was a confrontation.

576
00:45:00,229 --> 00:45:05,020
Once again, two Tomcat crews shot down two Libyan fighters with relative ease.

577
00:45:05,021 --> 00:45:08,620
That was actually almost Sidewinder 101.

578
00:45:08,621 --> 00:45:12,160
They've never really been challenged, and they'd like to be.

579
00:45:12,161 --> 00:45:18,300
Despite the F-14's superiority, experts felt by the end of the 1980s, the plane would eventually

580
00:45:18,301 --> 00:45:24,020
be phased out and replaced by newer fighter jets.

581
00:45:24,021 --> 00:45:33,020
But improvements made over that decade in the 1990s would extend the Tomcat's effectiveness.

582
00:45:33,021 --> 00:45:36,780
One of the most important upgrades involved the F-14's engine.

583
00:45:36,781 --> 00:45:43,780
The primary concern with the F-14 during its first decade of service was the possibility of engine compressor stalls.

584
00:45:45,240 --> 00:45:52,300
The earliest generation of engines was ideal for supersonic speeds, but not for extreme combat maneuvers.

585
00:45:53,080 --> 00:45:59,300
The airplane is a fighter, and as a fighter, there are a tremendous number of rapid throttle excursions.

586
00:46:00,100 --> 00:46:05,200
You had to watch carefully to make sure you didn't get too high angle of an attack, get too slow or whatever,

587
00:46:05,201 --> 00:46:08,700
get outside the allowable envelope.

588
00:46:08,701 --> 00:46:15,720
The stalls occurred at high altitudes and low speeds, triggered by irregular airflow.

589
00:46:15,721 --> 00:46:20,720
Left uncorrected, the aircraft could begin to spin uncontrollably.

590
00:46:20,721 --> 00:46:26,700
This scenario doomed some 19 F-14s in 1984.

591
00:46:26,701 --> 00:46:30,660
After the Navy installed the newer General Electric engines, pilots could fly through

592
00:46:30,661 --> 00:46:35,500
extreme angles of attack and maneuver without worrying about compressor stalls.

593
00:46:35,501 --> 00:46:43,100
The GE F110 engine really provided the thrust and the performance to the airplane that it

594
00:46:43,101 --> 00:46:44,780
had been originally designed for.

595
00:46:44,781 --> 00:46:51,700
The GE engine increased the afterburning thrust, total thrust in the airplane from about 21,000

596
00:46:51,701 --> 00:46:52,700
to over 28,000.

597
00:46:52,701 --> 00:46:56,180
This is about a 40% increase in thrust.

598
00:46:56,181 --> 00:47:02,580
It also provided a computer fuel control, which gave us stall-free operation throughout the envelope.

599
00:47:03,740 --> 00:47:09,420
In the early 90s, a new digital radar system gave the F-14 another performance boost.

600
00:47:10,020 --> 00:47:16,660
With the APG radar, the Tomcat could now track targets at nearly 200 miles from almost any altitude.

601
00:47:18,300 --> 00:47:26,140
And in 1995, the Lantern, developed by the Air Force, gave the F-14 laser-guided weapon capabilities.

602
00:47:26,180 --> 00:47:34,460
Tomcats used the Lantern for the first time in 1996 when they dropped precision-guided

603
00:47:34,461 --> 00:47:36,880
munitions over targets in Bosnia.

604
00:47:36,881 --> 00:47:40,880
This earned them the nickname Bombcats.

605
00:47:40,881 --> 00:47:47,420
The F-14 had shed its 70s-era technology, and it became a smarter, more effective aircraft.

606
00:47:47,421 --> 00:47:51,640
The original plane had only three computer processors.

607
00:47:51,641 --> 00:47:53,420
The latest versions have 33.

608
00:47:53,421 --> 00:48:00,520
So you always had your main central computer, but now you have distributed processors all over the airplane doing all kinds of calculations.

609
00:48:01,220 --> 00:48:09,960
But despite the Tomcat's wide variety of upgrades, military leaders began to determine that its days as America's top fighter jet were numbered.

610
00:48:11,060 --> 00:48:14,640
The time had come to make room for a new generation of combat aircraft.

611
00:48:15,580 --> 00:48:18,620
The proud old plane had a date with the junk man.

612
00:48:18,621 --> 00:48:26,500
Without weapons and fuel, the F-14 weighs 42,000 pounds.

613
00:48:26,501 --> 00:48:33,120
When fully loaded, its max weight can increase to more than 72,000 pounds.

614
00:48:33,121 --> 00:48:40,940
We'll be right back with more Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

615
00:48:40,941 --> 00:48:48,220
We now return to the special edition of Modern Marvels, the final farewell to the F-14 Tomcat.

616
00:48:48,221 --> 00:48:56,220
For three decades, the F-14 staked its claim as America's premier air defense fighter.

617
00:48:56,221 --> 00:49:03,220
After helping to win the Cold War, it reasserted its strategic stature in the skies above Bosnia,

618
00:49:03,221 --> 00:49:07,220
Kosovo, Afghanistan, and Iraq.

619
00:49:07,221 --> 00:49:14,220
But by the early 1990s, the Tomcat had become too difficult and expensive to maintain.

620
00:49:14,221 --> 00:49:19,580
Part of the problem was the complexity of its design, including its variable geometry wings.

621
00:49:20,760 --> 00:49:25,400
Another factor was the harsh salt air environment it inhabited on aircraft carriers.

622
00:49:26,340 --> 00:49:33,800
For every hour the F-14 was airborne, maintenance crews had to expend some 40 to 60 man hours to keep it operational.

623
00:49:33,801 --> 00:49:39,380
It's certainly still very capable, better than most airplanes in the world's inventory.

624
00:49:39,381 --> 00:49:44,380
has some awesome firepower to it, lethality to it.

625
00:49:44,381 --> 00:49:45,380
But it is an old airplane.

626
00:49:45,381 --> 00:49:48,380
The sailors that work on it work very, very hard

627
00:49:48,381 --> 00:49:49,380
to keep it up and keep it flying.

628
00:49:52,380 --> 00:49:56,380
We really started in earnest phasing out the F-14s

629
00:49:56,381 --> 00:50:00,380
in about the 1990, early 90s timeframe.

630
00:50:00,381 --> 00:50:02,380
The aircraft was approaching 20 years old.

631
00:50:02,381 --> 00:50:04,380
Many of the original aircraft were starting to reach

632
00:50:04,381 --> 00:50:06,380
the end of their service lives.

633
00:50:06,381 --> 00:50:09,380
So we started transitioning squadrons from two squadrons in each

634
00:50:09,381 --> 00:50:12,220
each battle group down to one squadron in each battle group.

635
00:50:12,221 --> 00:50:15,360
At the same time, the F-18 Hornet was coming online,

636
00:50:15,361 --> 00:50:17,280
so it was starting to take some of the missions

637
00:50:17,281 --> 00:50:19,380
that the F-14 could fly.

638
00:50:19,381 --> 00:50:21,360
The Navy finally decided to withdraw

639
00:50:21,361 --> 00:50:24,300
all remaining Tomcats by 2006.

640
00:50:25,360 --> 00:50:28,600
Each retired jet first comes to Oceana Naval Air Station

641
00:50:28,601 --> 00:50:29,440
in Virginia.

642
00:50:30,900 --> 00:50:33,580
Here, experts determine its ultimate fate.

643
00:50:34,480 --> 00:50:36,880
Some of those aircraft are destined to go to museums

644
00:50:36,881 --> 00:50:39,100
throughout the United States or military bases

645
00:50:39,101 --> 00:50:42,000
throughout the United States be placed on public display.

646
00:50:42,001 --> 00:50:44,840
Some of the aircraft will go to what we call AMARG,

647
00:50:44,841 --> 00:50:46,640
which is a Davis-Monthan Air Force base

648
00:50:46,641 --> 00:50:48,380
in Tucson, Arizona.

649
00:50:48,381 --> 00:50:51,140
That is a storage facility that military aircraft

650
00:50:51,141 --> 00:50:53,980
go out there in case we ever decided to bring a Tomcat

651
00:50:53,981 --> 00:50:55,520
back into service for some reason,

652
00:50:55,521 --> 00:50:58,980
we'll keep a certain number of Tomcats out there.

653
00:50:58,981 --> 00:51:02,080
The remaining F-14s stay here at Oceana,

654
00:51:02,081 --> 00:51:04,420
where teams of mechanics strip their parts

655
00:51:04,421 --> 00:51:06,520
in preparation for a date with the Shredder.

656
00:51:06,521 --> 00:51:14,520
It's all part of the Navy's Stricken Aircraft Reclamation and Disposal Program, or SARDIP.

657
00:51:14,521 --> 00:51:17,520
William Bell of Titan Systems spearheads the project.

658
00:51:17,521 --> 00:51:25,520
After 30 years of maintaining the F-14, being involved in the management and control of the aircraft,

659
00:51:25,521 --> 00:51:31,520
trying to extend the life as long as we possibly could, and seeing it in service for 30-plus years,

660
00:51:31,521 --> 00:51:37,320
It's a very bittersweet scenario for me to be the guy that also is in charge of making them go away.

661
00:51:38,460 --> 00:51:45,340
On this day, former F-14 maintenance crewmen Larry Brown, Matt Cullen, and Tyrone Pickett

662
00:51:45,341 --> 00:51:50,600
are stripping a Tomcat that once soared proudly over Iraq in Operation Iraqi Freedom.

663
00:51:51,840 --> 00:51:55,620
It's now known simply as number 161428.

664
00:51:56,820 --> 00:52:01,120
Each aircraft is assigned a viewer number, and for us, to make it easy for us,

665
00:52:01,121 --> 00:52:05,900
We just assigned to write the bureau number on the part and pretty much track it that

666
00:52:05,901 --> 00:52:06,900
way.

667
00:52:06,901 --> 00:52:11,760
It will take them about two to three weeks to carefully remove and catalog close to

668
00:52:11,761 --> 00:52:13,260
500 separate parts.

669
00:52:13,261 --> 00:52:18,440
I spent all those years maintaining them and now I'm actually tearing them apart.

670
00:52:18,441 --> 00:52:21,480
It's sort of hard to tell you the truth.

671
00:52:21,481 --> 00:52:23,080
I guess the first one was the worst.

672
00:52:23,081 --> 00:52:25,840
You know, just like anything else, you get used to it.

673
00:52:25,841 --> 00:52:30,720
We usually take out the ejection seats first because of the explosives in them.

674
00:52:30,721 --> 00:52:36,960
the avionics get all the confidential gear and things of that nature all the boxes out

675
00:52:38,720 --> 00:52:42,960
in this department here we'd have many of the radar type boxes weapons types boxes that would

676
00:52:42,961 --> 00:52:46,720
come out all of these type items and these are the type components that we were removed from

677
00:52:46,721 --> 00:52:51,520
the aircraft and those would go back into the naval supply system to be reused on other either

678
00:52:51,521 --> 00:52:55,920
other f-14s and some of these components may even fit other aircraft that they would reuse those on

679
00:52:55,921 --> 00:53:00,920
Each part stripped from the plane must be meticulously accounted for.

680
00:53:00,921 --> 00:53:04,920
This will help ensure that none of the parts falls into the wrong hands,

681
00:53:04,921 --> 00:53:10,920
like those of the Iranians, who still possess their fleet of inoperative F-14s from the 70s.

682
00:53:11,920 --> 00:53:20,120
With the Iranian situation and the Tomcats and the fact that we know that they would love to have a lot of our parts as we get rid of the F-14s to try to get theirs

683
00:53:20,121 --> 00:53:20,920
back in service,

684
00:53:20,921 --> 00:53:22,720
get theirs back in service.

685
00:53:22,721 --> 00:53:24,880
Control and accountability of our components

686
00:53:24,881 --> 00:53:27,320
during the sardic process is critical.

687
00:53:27,321 --> 00:53:29,480
Reduced to wingless carcasses,

688
00:53:29,481 --> 00:53:31,340
the Tomcat slated for destruction

689
00:53:31,341 --> 00:53:33,800
are unceremoniously towed to a site

690
00:53:33,801 --> 00:53:35,620
aptly known as the Boneyard.

691
00:53:36,840 --> 00:53:38,860
When it comes time to cut one up here,

692
00:53:38,861 --> 00:53:41,720
it's almost like losing a member of my family.

693
00:53:41,721 --> 00:53:43,540
It's been part of my life forever.

694
00:53:43,541 --> 00:53:53,540
The cut-up remains of the aluminum airframes are later shredded and recycled.

695
00:53:53,541 --> 00:53:59,580
You'll never know if the body of your new car or your can of soda once hurtled through

696
00:53:59,581 --> 00:54:05,420
the sky at Mach 2, defending America's interests.

697
00:54:05,421 --> 00:54:09,980
Whether an F-14 is scrapped or shipped to a museum, you can be sure that there is a

698
00:54:09,981 --> 00:54:14,760
crew member who had a tough time saying goodbye.

699
00:54:14,761 --> 00:54:20,160
As you deliver an airplane to a museum like that, you let the fuel burn itself out so

700
00:54:20,161 --> 00:54:21,420
you don't shut the engines off.

701
00:54:21,421 --> 00:54:25,140
You wait until they die, and it's really the final death of the airplane.

702
00:54:25,141 --> 00:54:30,200
And it kicks hard, and it chugs, and it tries to keep breathing and fighting.

703
00:54:30,201 --> 00:54:32,840
And boy, very emotional day.

704
00:54:32,841 --> 00:54:34,820
There's a lot of emotion out there.

705
00:54:34,821 --> 00:54:41,320
In September 2006, the final chapter on the venerable F-14 Tomcat came to a close as naval

706
00:54:41,321 --> 00:54:49,000
officials, former contractors, builders, pilots, crews, families and friends all had one last

707
00:54:49,001 --> 00:54:52,700
chance to bid the Tomcat farewell before it soared into history.

708
00:54:54,380 --> 00:55:00,560
We now return to the special edition of Modern Marvels, the final farewell to the F-14 Tomcat.

709
00:55:04,820 --> 00:55:11,820
Only a handful of F-14s remain on operational status at Naval Air Station Oceana in Virginia.

710
00:55:11,821 --> 00:55:17,820
The flight crews there are putting the last of these warbirds through their bases.

711
00:55:17,821 --> 00:55:23,820
Pilots, REOs, and ground crews are busy wrapping up final land-based flight operations

712
00:55:23,821 --> 00:55:29,820
before the last of the Tomcats fly off to various museums throughout the United States.

713
00:55:29,821 --> 00:55:35,360
The emotions are mixed as pilots celebrate their final ride with a traditional Navy champagne toast

714
00:55:35,940 --> 00:55:39,640
Ground crews go to work on this aircraft for the last time

715
00:55:42,400 --> 00:55:46,500
It's been sad I guess in my 24 years will be the saddest point of it

716
00:55:46,501 --> 00:55:48,340
We've seen the f-14 go

717
00:55:48,341 --> 00:55:52,580
But we know times moving on and the jet has to move on but it's still it's like

718
00:55:52,820 --> 00:55:56,000
It's like losing a little brother a little bit, you know, it's bittersweet

719
00:55:56,001 --> 00:55:57,740
It's gonna be sad, you know

720
00:55:57,741 --> 00:55:58,880
We put a lot of hours into it.

721
00:55:58,881 --> 00:56:01,440
There's guys here that work 14, 15 hours a day.

722
00:56:04,220 --> 00:56:07,560
In the coming days, Oceana will host a final flight ceremony

723
00:56:07,561 --> 00:56:11,120
to honor the F-14's long-lived career.

724
00:56:11,121 --> 00:56:14,980
As a courtesy, the Navy has invited me to attend the event.

725
00:56:14,981 --> 00:56:18,800
They've even offered to take me up in the Tomcat one last time.

726
00:56:21,500 --> 00:56:24,540
Now it's been 14 years since I've flown the F-14,

727
00:56:24,541 --> 00:56:27,500
but I haven't forgotten what an adrenaline rush it was

728
00:56:27,501 --> 00:56:30,740
to pilot this aircraft.

729
00:56:30,741 --> 00:56:33,320
But for this flight, I'll be riding shotgun in the Rio

730
00:56:33,321 --> 00:56:37,220
position, while Commander Kurt Seth, call sign Opie,

731
00:56:37,221 --> 00:56:40,220
does all the driving up front.

732
00:56:40,221 --> 00:56:43,900
So before I suit up, I'll need to review the backseat ejection

733
00:56:43,901 --> 00:56:46,640
and environmental systems on one of the Tomcats out

734
00:56:46,641 --> 00:56:49,380
on the flight line.

735
00:56:49,381 --> 00:56:51,020
When you come up here to the cockpit,

736
00:56:51,021 --> 00:56:52,600
you're going to have one safety pin right here,

737
00:56:52,601 --> 00:56:54,820
which keeps the ejection handle from pulling out.

738
00:56:54,821 --> 00:56:56,500
You're going to have one safety pin right here,

739
00:56:56,501 --> 00:56:58,460
which keeps the canopy jettison pin for pulling, yeah.

740
00:56:59,120 --> 00:57:01,420
With these pins in, the seat's safe, the canopy's safe.

741
00:57:02,060 --> 00:57:04,180
The lieutenant's going to strap you into the seat.

742
00:57:04,260 --> 00:57:05,660
Once he gets you inside the seat,

743
00:57:06,100 --> 00:57:08,560
he's going to remove the two safety pins from the two feet.

744
00:57:08,740 --> 00:57:11,360
If for some reason you need to go to an emergency auction supply,

745
00:57:11,460 --> 00:57:12,400
this is the last resort.

746
00:57:12,740 --> 00:57:13,780
Gilbert, thank you very much.

747
00:57:13,820 --> 00:57:14,520
Thank you, sir.

748
00:57:17,160 --> 00:57:20,060
When we were finished, it was time to head back to Flight Ops

749
00:57:20,061 --> 00:57:21,580
to suit up for the real deal.

750
00:57:24,300 --> 00:57:26,120
Now this part is a little tricky.

751
00:57:26,500 --> 00:57:30,420
It's like riding the bike, getting back in your stuff.

752
00:57:30,421 --> 00:57:32,940
Squeezing into the snug-fitting G-Suit and harness

753
00:57:32,941 --> 00:57:36,040
takes some practice.

754
00:57:36,041 --> 00:57:38,400
Once I had a good fit, the last piece of business

755
00:57:38,401 --> 00:57:41,100
was to clean the visor on my helmet

756
00:57:41,101 --> 00:57:43,040
and head out to the flight line with the XO.

757
00:57:47,080 --> 00:57:48,680
Together, we look the Tomcat over,

758
00:57:48,681 --> 00:57:51,940
inspecting all the pre-flight items.

759
00:57:51,941 --> 00:57:56,340
As usual, the plane captain has done an excellent job.

760
00:57:56,341 --> 00:58:03,340
Lieutenant Paul Dort, call sign Bonesaw, jumps in the cockpit and sets up the seat for my demonstration ride.

761
00:58:03,341 --> 00:58:09,340
In the meantime, I have a moment to soak in what's about to happen.

762
00:58:09,341 --> 00:58:17,340
PF14 Tomcat, by Ol' Bird. It's been 14 years. Half time, baby.

763
00:58:17,341 --> 00:58:26,100
It's go time, so I strap in, we drop the canopy, and roll out.

764
00:58:31,880 --> 00:58:34,680
So, 6-1, clear for takeoff on the right, switching airborne.

765
00:58:36,340 --> 00:58:37,880
Rudders, heights, motors, all good.

766
00:58:37,940 --> 00:58:38,720
No caution warnings.

767
00:58:39,020 --> 00:58:39,780
Flight instrument's good.

768
00:58:39,820 --> 00:58:40,520
I'm all set.

769
00:58:40,521 --> 00:58:41,221
Good to me.

770
00:58:41,222 --> 00:58:41,922
There we go.

771
00:58:47,340 --> 00:58:54,340
Now, only 3,500 feet of runway stand before me and my Mach 1 sprint down memory lane.

772
00:58:54,341 --> 00:58:56,340
That's beautiful.

773
00:59:00,340 --> 00:59:06,340
As soon as we got airborne, Commander Seth reintroduced me to the intensity of G-Force

774
00:59:06,341 --> 00:59:11,340
by pulling a couple of barrel rolls and a few evasive maneuvers.

775
00:59:11,341 --> 00:59:13,340
Okay, take one.

776
00:59:17,340 --> 00:59:19,340
And we'll go right.

777
00:59:19,341 --> 00:59:21,340
Nice.

778
00:59:21,341 --> 00:59:22,340
Speed forward, see.

779
00:59:22,341 --> 00:59:24,340
Very nice.

780
00:59:24,341 --> 00:59:33,340
Before we headed back, Opie plugged the afterburners and accelerated the bird to Supersonic.

781
00:59:33,341 --> 00:59:37,340
All right, here we go, Supersonic dash.

782
00:59:37,341 --> 00:59:38,340
Hear him?

783
00:59:38,341 --> 00:59:39,340
Okay.

784
00:59:39,341 --> 00:59:40,340
We're spinning safely.

785
00:59:40,341 --> 00:59:42,340
If we can get there, there's 119.

786
00:59:42,341 --> 00:59:43,340
There it is.

787
00:59:43,341 --> 00:59:44,340
Come on, baby.

788
00:59:44,341 --> 00:59:49,340
Come on baby. There it is, 1.2. Nice.

789
00:59:56,340 --> 00:59:59,340
Upon our return to base, we came in for a touch and go.

790
00:59:59,341 --> 01:00:04,340
Throttling up to full power, we took to the wild blue and circled around again.

791
01:00:10,340 --> 01:00:13,340
On final approach, the XO put her down for good.

792
01:00:14,340 --> 01:00:17,500
and we taxied in.

793
01:00:23,500 --> 01:00:24,620
That was awesome.

794
01:00:24,980 --> 01:00:26,560
That was unbelievable.

795
01:00:26,920 --> 01:00:28,180
It's everything I remembered.

796
01:00:29,100 --> 01:00:30,460
I'd do it again in a heartbeat.

797
01:00:32,140 --> 01:00:33,640
XO, thank you very much.

798
01:00:33,800 --> 01:00:35,080
You did a great job.

799
01:00:35,300 --> 01:00:36,380
Couldn't have been better.

800
01:00:38,020 --> 01:00:39,940
For a retired Tomcat pilot,

801
01:00:40,300 --> 01:00:43,300
walking away from the F-14 for a second last time

802
01:00:43,301 --> 01:00:44,480
felt pretty amazing.

803
01:00:48,080 --> 01:00:49,700
But on the following day,

804
01:00:49,980 --> 01:00:52,300
the Navy only had one shot to say goodbye.

805
01:00:53,180 --> 01:00:54,420
To kick off the ceremony,

806
01:00:54,700 --> 01:00:55,900
a memorial was unveiled

807
01:00:55,901 --> 01:00:58,320
at the Oceana Warbird Petting Zoo,

808
01:00:58,540 --> 01:00:59,240
as they call it.

809
01:00:59,780 --> 01:01:01,580
Many of the Tomcat's biggest fans

810
01:01:01,581 --> 01:01:02,740
took to the podium.

811
01:01:02,741 --> 01:01:03,700
One model of aircraft.

812
01:01:04,980 --> 01:01:06,280
We all have our memories

813
01:01:06,281 --> 01:01:07,880
of this magnificent fighter.

814
01:01:08,720 --> 01:01:09,920
Those memories will continue

815
01:01:09,921 --> 01:01:11,080
to feel the fighter's spirit

816
01:01:11,081 --> 01:01:12,160
for decades to come.

817
01:01:13,300 --> 01:01:21,060
Over on the tarmac, a crowd of naval officials, former contractors, pilots, crews, families

818
01:01:21,061 --> 01:01:29,060
and friends gather to share stories and to witness the F-14's final flight into history.

819
01:01:29,061 --> 01:01:32,700
The F-14 Tomcat certainly has a great legacy.

820
01:01:32,701 --> 01:01:38,660
It's certainly a legend in naval aviation, and it's more than earned its place in history

821
01:01:38,661 --> 01:01:42,260
when you really stand back and look across the history of aerospace.

822
01:01:42,261 --> 01:01:46,680
I took many young aviators to various aircraft carriers on the West Coast,

823
01:01:46,920 --> 01:01:52,000
and I never, ever lost the thrill of seeing the light bulb come on

824
01:01:52,001 --> 01:01:55,660
for somebody in the front of a very difficult airplane to land on board an aircraft carrier.

825
01:01:56,220 --> 01:01:58,580
And it motivated me. I loved doing it.

826
01:01:59,640 --> 01:02:01,440
I did it probably more than I should have.

827
01:02:02,620 --> 01:02:07,280
But it was really, as I look back on all the things I've gotten to do in the F-14,

828
01:02:07,460 --> 01:02:09,420
I still hold that as the highlight.

829
01:02:09,421 --> 01:02:16,420
As I was mingling with the crowd of Tomcat enthusiasts, I ran into a couple of familiar faces.

830
01:02:16,421 --> 01:02:19,420
Miramar fighter buddies from way back.

831
01:02:19,421 --> 01:02:22,420
Two of the most talented fighter pilots I've ever flown with in my career.

832
01:02:22,421 --> 01:02:24,420
And I'm honored to have hung with these guys.

833
01:02:24,421 --> 01:02:28,420
He's the second best fighter pilot, well actually third best fighter pilot I know.

834
01:02:28,421 --> 01:02:30,420
One and two.

835
01:02:30,421 --> 01:02:35,420
Once the ceremony began, all eyes were on the Tomcat.

836
01:02:35,421 --> 01:02:46,680
So please join me in a grand naval tradition by standing and offering three cheers and farewell to the Tomcat.

837
01:02:47,500 --> 01:02:53,280
Hip hip hooray! Hip hip hooray! Hip hip hooray!

838
01:02:53,281 --> 01:03:00,280
And with those final words on September 22, 2006,

839
01:03:00,281 --> 01:03:03,280
at Naval Air Station Oceana in Virginia,

840
01:03:03,281 --> 01:03:10,280
the Navy bid farewell to a feared, revered, and trusted friend forever.

841
01:03:10,281 --> 01:03:14,280
Ladies and gentlemen, the last flight of the F-14 Tomcat.

842
01:03:23,280 --> 01:03:30,980
I'm standing here at the Aerospace Maintenance and Regeneration Center, better known as the

843
01:03:30,981 --> 01:03:32,320
Boneyard.

844
01:03:32,321 --> 01:03:36,560
It's just outside Tucson, Arizona, wedged between Davis-Month and Air Force Base, and

845
01:03:36,561 --> 01:03:39,600
miles of dry desert sand.

846
01:03:39,601 --> 01:03:46,280
It's the perfect environment for veteran warbirds like the Tomcat to retire.

847
01:03:46,281 --> 01:03:53,780
Amark is the final resting place for more than 4,200 aircraft, including 167 F-14 Tomcats.

848
01:03:56,180 --> 01:03:59,560
Most aviators consider this place hallowed ground.

849
01:04:00,200 --> 01:04:03,380
It's a sanctuary filled with distinguished veteran moorbirds.

850
01:04:05,220 --> 01:04:12,320
A lot of aircraft here at Amark, the Boneyard, are here in some sort of mothballed condition

851
01:04:12,321 --> 01:04:15,220
that can be recalled at the drop of a hat?

852
01:04:15,400 --> 01:04:16,100
Correct.

853
01:04:16,101 --> 01:04:20,140
A lot of the aircraft that are here are stored so that we can pull them back into flying service if we need to,

854
01:04:20,240 --> 01:04:25,140
and historically about 21% of aircraft that come in do fly again in one capacity or another.

855
01:04:25,620 --> 01:04:30,580
The F-4 Phantoms, predecessor for these guys, you see flying around now is target drones come back out.

856
01:04:31,000 --> 01:04:32,340
But also a lot of parts birds.

857
01:04:32,440 --> 01:04:37,580
So an aircraft like this that looks like it's been stripped down has saved the taxpayer millions of dollars

858
01:04:37,581 --> 01:04:41,720
in parts that have come off that aircraft and gone back out in a fleet that we haven't had to buy new.

859
01:04:41,721 --> 01:04:48,720
Can you give us a quick rundown on exactly how an F-14 comes in here, how is it mothballed?

860
01:04:48,721 --> 01:04:49,720
What does that process entail?

861
01:04:49,721 --> 01:04:53,720
Quick step, aircraft comes in, Navy and Marine Corps aircraft get washed.

862
01:04:53,721 --> 01:04:56,720
We want to make sure we get all the salt water off them because they're working in a saltwater environment.

863
01:04:56,721 --> 01:04:59,720
Get that cleaned off the aircraft, then the aircraft goes and gets defueled.

864
01:04:59,721 --> 01:05:03,720
We drain the fuel out of it, we'll refuel it with a lightweight oil, it's like a sewing machine oil.

865
01:05:03,721 --> 01:05:07,720
We'll burn that through it, run that through the engines, that preserves the fuel system.

866
01:05:07,721 --> 01:05:10,720
Then we'll wash it again, then we take it over and put this spray lat on it.

867
01:05:10,721 --> 01:05:12,720
That's this white material that you see on it.

868
01:05:12,721 --> 01:05:17,720
And that starts out with black electrician's tape to seal cracks, crevices, access panels.

869
01:05:17,721 --> 01:05:19,720
Then it gets double coat of the black material.

870
01:05:19,721 --> 01:05:23,720
The black material seals the aircraft, and then the white material goes on it,

871
01:05:23,721 --> 01:05:27,720
because it's living now in Tucson, Arizona, 110 degrees in the summer.

872
01:05:27,721 --> 01:05:29,720
And so the white material functions like roof coating.

873
01:05:29,721 --> 01:05:34,720
It basically keeps it cool inside at about 10 to 15 degrees above ambient air temperature.

874
01:05:34,721 --> 01:05:39,720
712 F-14s were built for the U.S. Navy.

875
01:05:39,721 --> 01:05:42,720
Today, only a fraction of those still exist.

876
01:05:42,721 --> 01:05:46,720
Most of the older ones have already been stripped for parts.

877
01:05:46,721 --> 01:05:51,720
A few, however, look as if they haven't aged a day.

878
01:05:51,721 --> 01:05:55,720
We've been lucky enough to find Myall Squadron, VF-51,

879
01:05:55,721 --> 01:05:56,720
the Screaming Eagles.

880
01:05:56,721 --> 01:05:58,720
I think it's the only one left out here.

881
01:05:58,721 --> 01:06:02,720
The squadron was disestablished in 1995 or so,

882
01:06:02,721 --> 01:06:04,720
and that's when this jet arrived out here,

883
01:06:04,721 --> 01:06:09,720
and up until that point was one of the oldest fighter squadrons in the country.

884
01:06:09,721 --> 01:06:11,720
We were based at NAS Miramar in San Diego.

885
01:06:11,721 --> 01:06:14,720
It was a great time to be a Screaming Eagle.

886
01:06:17,720 --> 01:06:20,720
Aircrafts slated for reclamation here at AMARC

887
01:06:20,721 --> 01:06:23,720
will be treated and stored for at least four years.

888
01:06:23,721 --> 01:06:25,720
The latest arrivals can be found on the flight line,

889
01:06:25,721 --> 01:06:30,720
where the process begins under a canopy shelter.

890
01:06:30,721 --> 01:06:36,800
This is it, the last F-14 Tomcat models flown into Davis-Monthan here in the Boneyard.

891
01:06:36,801 --> 01:06:41,820
They're F-14D models, the newest Navy's models, probably only 14 years old.

892
01:06:42,000 --> 01:06:45,980
They belong to VF-31, the Tomcatters, based at NAS Oceana,

893
01:06:46,240 --> 01:06:49,360
and embarked aboard the Teddy Roosevelt, CVN-71.

894
01:06:51,040 --> 01:06:56,760
It's a long shot, but it's possible these retired fighters might get called back to active duty in the future.

895
01:06:56,761 --> 01:07:03,760
But until then, the Tomcats here at the Boneyard will have to quietly wait to fight another day.

896
01:07:06,760 --> 01:07:09,760
A new breed of Tomcat fighter now rules the skies.

897
01:07:11,760 --> 01:07:16,760
But the F-14 will endure as the pioneering wonder that changed the face of aerial warfare.

898
01:07:17,760 --> 01:07:22,760
A plane not measured simply by its superior abilities, but also by its indelible mystique.

899
01:07:22,761 --> 01:07:25,760
I'm Terry Deets for the History Channel.

900
01:07:25,761 --> 01:07:29,760
Thanks for watching.

